1
00:00:01,292 --> 00:00:02,500
Hallo. This is Liam, in for Bear.

2
00:00:02,958 --> 00:00:05,292
A slow agent does not necessarily need a
shorter

3
00:00:05,292 --> 00:00:07,333
prompt. It needs a better division of
work.

4
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:09,208
Let's separate skills, tools, and
subagents.

5
00:00:10,542 --> 00:00:12,875
Three different things. A skill supplies
instructions.

6
00:00:13,292 --> 00:00:14,583
A tool performs an action.

7
00:00:14,958 --> 00:00:17,083
A subagent works in a separate context.

8
00:00:17,458 --> 00:00:19,583
A skill can guide tool use, but that does

9
00:00:19,583 --> 00:00:21,583
not automatically make it a separate
agent.

10
00:00:22,375 --> 00:00:25,708
Imagine putting every procedure into the
agent's starting instructions.

11
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,500
Forecasting, checking inventory, writing a
report.

12
00:00:28,833 --> 00:00:30,958
Most tasks need only some of that
material.

13
00:00:31,541 --> 00:00:33,958
The first design question is what belongs
in the

14
00:00:33,958 --> 00:00:35,958
core, and what can wait until it is
needed.

15
00:00:37,958 --> 00:00:40,291
Anthropic's StockPilot workshop reports
reducing its system prompt

16
00:00:40,291 --> 00:00:42,750
from four hundred and two lines to
fifteen.

17
00:00:42,791 --> 00:00:44,583
The other instructions did not disappear.

18
00:00:45,125 --> 00:00:47,958
Roughly four hundred lines moved into
skills, loaded on

19
00:00:47,958 --> 00:00:50,708
demand. That is deferred context, not
deleted knowledge.

20
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:53,291
Here is a skill being used.

21
00:00:53,458 --> 00:00:56,250
The agent loads the relevant instructions
into its existing

22
00:00:56,250 --> 00:00:58,875
context. The page joins the current work.

23
00:00:59,041 --> 00:01:01,166
By default, this is not a fresh worker
with

24
00:01:01,166 --> 00:01:04,291
a private memory. Separate execution has
to be configured

25
00:01:04,291 --> 00:01:04,958
explicitly.

26
00:01:05,791 --> 00:01:07,166
Now the agent calls a tool.

27
00:01:07,458 --> 00:01:09,625
A request goes out; a result comes back.

28
00:01:09,833 --> 00:01:12,416
That tool might read inventory, run code,
or write

29
00:01:12,416 --> 00:01:15,791
a file. Tools are not automatically
harmless or stateless.

30
00:01:16,208 --> 00:01:18,583
What they can change depends on the
implementation and

31
00:01:18,583 --> 00:01:18,958
permissions.

32
00:01:19,916 --> 00:01:21,083
A subagent is different.

33
00:01:21,333 --> 00:01:23,208
Give a worker a bounded task and its own

34
00:01:23,208 --> 00:01:25,833
context. It does the work there, then
returns a

35
00:01:25,833 --> 00:01:28,666
result. This can keep intermediate detail
out of the

36
00:01:28,666 --> 00:01:32,166
parent's conversation. It can also add
coordination time and

37
00:01:32,166 --> 00:01:33,375
additional model usage.

38
00:01:34,208 --> 00:01:35,583
Try an inventory assistant.

39
00:01:35,791 --> 00:01:37,666
A skill explains the reorder policy.

40
00:01:38,041 --> 00:01:39,583
A tool reads the stock record.

41
00:01:39,833 --> 00:01:42,125
The agent uses both to draft a
recommendation.

42
00:01:42,541 --> 00:01:45,000
The instruction page tells it how to
decide; the

43
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:46,625
tool result supplies evidence.

44
00:01:46,916 --> 00:01:48,125
Neither replaces the other.

45
00:01:48,875 --> 00:01:51,125
Before placing an order, put a human
approval point

46
00:01:51,125 --> 00:01:52,500
between the draft and the action.

47
00:01:52,958 --> 00:01:55,083
Check the quantity, supplier, and
evidence.

48
00:01:55,791 --> 00:01:58,375
Reading stock and spending money should
not inherit the

49
00:01:58,375 --> 00:02:01,208
same permission merely because they sit
inside the same

50
00:02:01,208 --> 00:02:01,583
workflow.

51
00:02:02,416 --> 00:02:04,875
The workshop also changed how data was
processed.

52
00:02:05,250 --> 00:02:06,958
Its daily sweep went from a hundred and
two

53
00:02:06,958 --> 00:02:08,541
tool calls to three scripts.

54
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:10,875
Those scripts worked over data directly.

55
00:02:11,375 --> 00:02:13,916
Moving instructions into skills was only
one part of

56
00:02:13,916 --> 00:02:15,916
the redesign; batching work was another.

57
00:02:16,708 --> 00:02:19,291
For that daily sweep, the workshop reports
four hundred

58
00:02:19,333 --> 00:02:22,125
and eighty-eight seconds before, and about
one hundred seconds

59
00:02:22,125 --> 00:02:24,666
after. Several changes contributed.

60
00:02:25,083 --> 00:02:27,500
Treat this as a reported example, not a
guaranteed

61
00:02:27,875 --> 00:02:29,250
fivefold speedup for your agent.

62
00:02:29,583 --> 00:02:31,375
We have not rerun that benchmark here.

63
00:02:32,041 --> 00:02:33,916
Test your redesign on the same tasks.

64
00:02:34,166 --> 00:02:36,208
Check correctness, elapsed time, and
usage.

65
00:02:36,666 --> 00:02:39,083
If the result is faster but wrong, reject
it.

66
00:02:39,416 --> 00:02:41,875
If a separate worker returns a huge
transcript, you

67
00:02:41,875 --> 00:02:42,916
may just have moved the clutter.

68
00:02:43,416 --> 00:02:45,875
Measure the complete workflow, not the
prompt's line count.

69
00:02:46,625 --> 00:02:48,958
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: Split
my workflow

70
00:02:48,958 --> 00:02:51,458
into instructions, actions, and separate
workers.

71
00:02:51,791 --> 00:02:54,666
Justify each boundary. Then check two
things yourself.

72
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:56,166
Can every action be tested?

73
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:58,541
Does every separate worker need its own
context?

74
00:02:58,541 --> 00:03:00,291
If not, simplify the design.

75
00:03:02,375 --> 00:03:03,583
Agent Decomposition: Skills vs Tools.

76
00:03:03,583 --> 00:03:04,750
At Nik Bear Brown.
